# TOPIC NAME

> **Template** for a "Learning about Claude" reference doc — a slice of the
> Claude Code docs recast in a form the reader absorbs: a plain ELI5 layer
> over a conceptual model, plus practical usage, layered generic → specific.
> Copy to `config/claude/docs/<topic>.md`, then replace every `>` guidance
> note and `PLACEHOLDER`. `LOOPS-WORKFLOWS.md` is the worked example of
> every section below.
>
> **Conventions:** GitHub-flavored Markdown; wrap prose at 78 columns;
> reference-style links only, all defined at the **bottom** (no inline URLs,
> no "distilled from" links in the abstract); markdownlint-clean — every
> link definition must be **used** (unused ones fail MD053), every used
> label defined; emphasis uses asterisks (`*italic*`, `**bold**`). Delete
> any section a topic doesn't need rather than leaving it empty; the ones
> marked **optional** are the usual candidates.
>
> **Abstract** — 3–6 sentences, **link-free**, no command list. What the
> subject IS and what this doc covers, in plain terms; write it so it can be
> lifted verbatim into a table of contents. Bold the core term(s) on first
> use.

## ELI5

> Plain-language one-liners for every command / concept the doc names,
> grouped by **intent** (what the reader is trying to do) — this doubles as
> a quick summary, so keep the "ELI5" header even though it is more than an
> ELI5. End with a one-line, linked pointer to any adjacent, out-of-scope
> family.

*Intent group A (e.g. "make it do X"):*

- **`/command`** — what it does, in one plain sentence.
- **concept** — what it is, in one plain sentence.

*Intent group B:*

- ...

### Best practices

> The do / don't guidance for this topic; each bullet an actionable rule of
> thumb.

- **Rule of thumb** — the why, briefly.
- ...

## Overview

> The **conceptual model**: what the pieces are and the single axis /
> distinction that organizes them (the "aha" that makes the rest click).
> End with the key distinction that sets up the deeper sections — the
> sentence the reader should remember.

### At a glance

> A table of the pieces with the columns that carry the model (what varies
> across them). Immediately follow it with prose that walks the rows **in
> the same order as the table**, one clause each — the table shows the axes,
> the prose gives the feel.

| Piece | AXIS 1 | AXIS 2 | Reach for it when |
|-------|--------|--------|-------------------|
| **piece 1** | ... | ... | ... |
| **piece 2** | ... | ... | ... |

In table order: **piece 1** ...; **piece 2** ...; one clause per piece, in
the table's order.

## MAJOR GROUP A

> A major grouping of the pieces (in the example doc: "Loops" and
> "Workflows"). Open with a one-line framing of what unifies this group,
> then one `###` per piece that belongs to it, **in the same order as the
> at-a-glance table**. Keep each piece in exactly one group.
>
> Single-topic doc with no natural split? Drop this MAJOR GROUP layer and
> make each piece a top-level `##` instead.

### Piece — deeper explanation

> What it is, how it behaves, when to use it, and the gotchas. Add
> sub-tables, examples, and code fences as needed.

### Next piece

...

## MAJOR GROUP B

> Same shape as GROUP A, for the other group. Add more `##` groups only if
> the topic genuinely has them.

### Piece

...

## Bringing it together

> **Optional** — skip for a simple single-topic doc. Use it when the pieces
> **compose**: how they combine, the decision of when to reach for which, a
> worked end-to-end example. This is where the doc earns its keep over the
> raw reference.

## See also — adjacent, out of scope

> **Optional.** Related families this doc deliberately does **not** cover —
> one bullet each, a link, and one line on why it is a different thing.
> Keeps the doc focused while pointing the reader onward.

- **Adjacent thing** — what it is and why it's separate. See [name][label].

